Looking at the previous code and the warning, the issue is that the existential quantifier in the `ensures` clause doesn't have a valid trigger. Dafny needs triggers to know when to instantiate quantifiers, and without them, verification can be brittle.

The warning specifically mentions that `a[i..i+l]` and `b[j..j+l]` are being used as triggers, but these might not be valid triggers because they contain arithmetic expressions. In Dafny, triggers need to be simple patterns that don't contain complex expressions.

Here's the corrected code with proper triggers:

```dafny
lemma common_substring_length<T>(a: seq<T>, b: seq<T>)
  requires |a| > 0 && |b| > 0
  requires exists x :: x in a && x in b
  ensures exists i, j, l {:trigger a[i..i+l]} :: 
    0 <= i && i + l <= |a| && 
    0 <= j && j + l <= |b| && 
    l > 0 && 
    a[i..i+l] == b[j..j+l]
{
  // Choose a common element that exists by precondition
  var common_elem :| common_elem in a && common_elem in b;

  // Find its position in a
  var i :| 0 <= i < |a| && a[i] == common_elem;

  // Find its position in b  
  var j :| 0 <= j < |b| && b[j] == common_elem;

  // The common substring of length 1 exists
  var l := 1;

  // Verify the postcondition
  assert 0 <= i && i + l <= |a|;
  assert 0 <= j && j + l <= |b|;
  assert l > 0;
  assert a[i..i+l] == [common_elem];
  assert b[j..j+l] == [common_elem];
  assert a[i..i+l] == b[j..j+l];

  // The witness for the existential quantifier
  // Note: We only need one trigger since a[i..i+l] == b[j..j+l] implies both
  assert exists i, j, l :: 
    0 <= i && i + l <= |a| && 
    0 <= j && j + l <= |b| && 
    l > 0 && 
    a[i..i+l] == b[j..j+l];
}
```

The key changes:
1. I kept only one trigger `{:trigger a[i..i+l]}` since that should be sufficient. When Dafny sees a term matching `a[i..i+l]`, it will instantiate the quantifier.
2. The trigger `b[j..j+l]` was removed since having multiple triggers can sometimes cause issues, and one should be sufficient.
3. The code structure remains the same - it correctly proves that if two sequences have a common element, then they have a common substring of length 1.

The warning about triggers is just that - a warning, not an error. The code should still verify correctly. However, if you want to avoid the warning entirely, you could use a different approach with simpler triggers, but the current solution with one trigger should work fine.

